  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">GPPAC Core Support 2021-2024</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">GPPAC k&#xe4;rnst&#xf6;d 2021-2024</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">The Global Partnership for the Prevention of Armed Conflict (GPPAC) har f&#xe5;tt k&#xe4;rnst&#xf6;d f&#xf6;r perioden juli 2021 - december 2023 som st&#xf6;d i genomf&#xf6;randet av organisationens strategiska plan &quot;Building Peace Together: Globalising Local Voices, Localising Global Commitments&quot; 2021-2025. St&#xf6;det &#xe4;r f&#xf6;rl&#xe4;ngt f&#xf6;r perioden 1 januari - 30 december 2024. 

GPPAC &#xe4;r ett globalt n&#xe4;tverk av civilsamh&#xe4;llesorganisationer som aktivt verkar f&#xf6;r att f&#xf6;rhindra v&#xe5;ldsam konflikt och bygga mer fredliga samh&#xe4;llen. N&#xe4;tverket best&#xe5;r av 15 regionala n&#xe4;tverk, var och ett med sina egna prioriteringar, karakt&#xe4;r och agenda - utifr&#xe5;n den regionala kontexten och behov. GPPAC har f&#xf6;r n&#xe4;rvarande cirka 240 medlemmar, vilket g&#xf6;r organisationen till v&#xe4;rldens st&#xf6;rsta globala n&#xe4;tverk av lokala fredsbyggare. GPPAC:s &#xf6;vergripande m&#xe5;l f&#xf6;r dess strategiska plan &quot;Building Peace Together: Globalising Local Voices, Localising Global Commitments&quot; 2021-2025 &#xe4;r att policy, praktik och infrastruktur ska vara f&#xf6;rankrade i lokalt drivet och lokalt grundat fredsbyggande med fokus p&#xe5; tre tematiska omr&#xe5;den: 
1. Lokalt ledd fredsbyggande verksamhet
2. Inkluderande av fredsbyggare som &#xe4;r kvinnor och unga
3. Klimats&#xe4;kerhet och begynnande hot</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Global Partnership for the Prevention of Armed Conflict (GPPAC) has received core support for the period July 2021 - December 2023 in support of the organisation&apos;s Strategic Plan  &quot;Building Peace Together: Globalising Local Voices, Localising Global Commitments&quot; 2021-2025. The support is extended for the period 1 January - 30 December 2024. 

GPPAC is a global network of civil society organisations (CSOs) actively working to prevent violent conflict and build more peaceful societies. The network consists of 15 regional networks, each with its own priorities, character, and agenda &#x2013; tailored to the regional context and needs.&#xa0;GPPAC currently has around 240 members, which makes the organisation the world&#x2019;s largest global network of local peacebuilders. GPPAC&apos;s overarching aim for its strategic plan is for policies, practices and infrastructures to be rooted in locally-led and locally-grounded peacebuilding with a focus on three thematic areas:
1. Locally-led Peacebuilding Action
2. Inclusion of Women and Youth Peacebuilders
3. Climate Security and Emerging Threats</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The overarching aim for the Global Partnership for the Prevention of Armed Conflict (GPPAC), a global network of civil society organisations (CSOs), and its strategic plan &quot;Building Peace Together: Globalising Local Voices, Localising Global Commitments&quot; 2021-2025 is for policies, practices and infrastructures to be rooted in locally-led and locally-grounded peacebuilding with a focus on three thematic areas:&#xa0;
1. Locally-led Peacebuilding Action - with a focus on both building and sustaining inclusive infrastructures for peace centred around local ownership, and facilitating multi-stakeholder engagements that explore and promote innovative ways of increasing accessible funding for local peacebuilders.
2. Inclusion of Women and Youth Peacebuilders -&#xa0;strengthening the capacity of the GPPAC network for inclusive peacebuilding and conflict prevention; by supporting the network&apos;s gender and youth experts in strategically engaging in peacebuilding processes at every level; and by ensuring that members contribute to further develop and implement global policies and frameworks.&#xa0;
3. Climate Security and Emerging Threats -&#xa0;by capitalising on the expertise and experience of GPPAC members. Through cross-regional learning, GPPAC aims to build the knowledge and understanding of GPPAC members to mainstream a peacebuilding response to climate-related risks and other emerging threats in programming, and influence power holders to do the same.
To achieve this, GPPAC foresees working through what it describes as three Pathways of Change. Firstly, to amplify member expertise by strengthening the network&apos;s platforms for exchange of expertise and learning and to facilitate organisational development within regional networks. Secondly, to mobilise collective action by&#xa0;enhancing collaboration and joint action between members at all levels. And thirdly, to influence policy and practice by building capacity and create space for meaningful engagement of local peacebuilders at the regional and international levels to raise visibility of and gain support for local peacebuilding work.</narrative>
  </description>
